= OODA loop — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''OODA''' stands for '''observe, orient, decide, act'''. It is John Boyd's way of describing how people and organizations can learn and respond while a situation changes. The important part is not racing through four boxes: it is continually updating how you understand the situation.<ref name="BoydSimple">John R. Boyd, ''The Essence of Winning and Losing'' (1995), reproduced in ''A Discourse on Winning and Losing'' (Air University Press, 2018), appendix. [https://media.defense.gov/2018/May/22/2001920668/-1/-1/0/B_0151_Boyd_Discourse_Winning_Losing.pdf Official edition].</ref>

== A practical example ==
Imagine a team responding to a service outage.

# '''Observe:''' users report failed requests; monitoring shows errors.
# '''Orient:''' the team compares the signals with what it knows about recent changes and previous incidents.
# '''Decide:''' it chooses a reversible mitigation, such as rolling back a suspected release.
# '''Act:''' it applies the change and checks whether errors actually decline.

If new evidence contradicts the team's explanation, it revises its understanding. It need not wait to finish a rigid cycle before noticing something important.

== What people often miss ==
'''Orientation''' means the interpretation of information, not just collecting it. Assumptions can be wrong. Boyd's original drawing contains several feedback paths and shortcuts; the familiar four-word slogan is only a simplification.

== What it does not promise ==
The OODA loop does not guarantee success or establish that faster decisions are always better. A fast response based on bad assumptions may make a problem worse.

